    instead of logging in. Gets the interesting design work without burning the project on auth plumbing. Auth slots in
    later without touching the notification design.

  [1mMy recommendation: multi-user with fake auth.[0m Your phrasing — "users," plural, "tasks they care about" — describes
  collaboration, and a single-user reminder system wouldn't need the word "notified" at all. But real auth is the least
  interesting and most replaceable part, so I'd stub it and spend the design effort on the event model.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Does more than one person share these tasks? → Multi-user, fake auth (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: what makes a task one you "care about." This decides whether interest is [3mderived[0m from task fields or [3mstored[0m as
  its own thing.

  - [1mImplicit only[0m — you're notified about tasks you created or are assigned to. Nothing extra to store; interest falls
    out of the task row. Simplest, but you can't follow a task that isn't yours, and you can't stop hearing about one
    that is.
  - [1mImplicit + explicit follow[0m — the above, plus a "watch" button and the ability to unfollow something assigned to you.
    Needs a real subscriptions table. This is what GitHub, Linear, and Jira all converge on, and adding it later means
    backfilling subscriptions for every existing task.
  - [1mExplicit only[0m — you hear nothing until you opt in. Clean model, but new users get an empty feed and miss things
    assigned to them, which reads as broken.

  [1mRecommendation: implicit + explicit follow.[0m The subscriptions table is cheap to build now and awkward to retrofit, and
  "unfollow this noisy task" is the first thing anyone asks for.
